# Cursor Rules for Debsoc2.0

## Code Optimization
- All code must be optimized for performance, readability, and maintainability
- Use React best practices: memoization, proper hooks usage, and efficient re-renders
- Leverage Next.js features (server components, static generation, etc.) when appropriate
- Minimize bundle size by avoiding unnecessary dependencies
- Use TypeScript strictly - no `any` types unless absolutely necessary
- Follow DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principles
- Optimize images and assets appropriately

## Preserve Existing Functionality
- **NEVER override or break existing code and functionality**
- Before making changes, thoroughly understand the existing codebase structure
- When modifying files, preserve all existing features, logic, and behavior
- Maintain backward compatibility with existing APIs and components
- Test that existing functionality still works after any changes
- If refactoring is needed, do it incrementally without breaking changes
- Preserve existing prop interfaces, function signatures, and data structures
- Keep existing state management patterns and data flow

## Design Consistency
- **Maintain and match the existing website design**
- Follow the established design system, color palette, and typography
- Use existing Tailwind CSS classes and custom CSS variables
- Match existing component styles, spacing, and layout patterns
- Preserve animation patterns (Framer Motion, GSAP) and timing
- Maintain responsive design breakpoints and mobile-first approach
- Keep consistent with existing UI/UX patterns and interactions
- Follow the same visual hierarchy and component structure
- Use existing design tokens from globals.css and theme configuration

## Responsive Design
- **ALL code must be fully responsive and work across all standard screen sizes**
- Test and ensure compatibility with:
  - Mobile devices (320px - 768px): phones and small tablets
  - Tablets (768px - 1024px): iPad and similar devices
  - Laptops (1024px - 1440px): standard laptop screens
  - Desktops (1440px+): large desktop monitors
- Use Tailwind CSS responsive breakpoints (sm, md, lg, xl, 2xl) consistently
- Implement mobile-first approach: design for mobile, then enhance for larger screens
- Ensure all components, layouts, and UI elements adapt properly to different screen sizes
- Test text readability and font sizes across all breakpoints
- Ensure images and media scale appropriately without breaking layouts
- Verify touch targets are appropriately sized for mobile devices (minimum 44x44px)
- Maintain consistent spacing and padding across all screen sizes
- Ensure navigation and menus work seamlessly on mobile and desktop
- Test horizontal scrolling is avoided on all screen sizes
- Use flexible layouts (flexbox, grid) that adapt to container sizes
- Ensure modals, dropdowns, and overlays work correctly on all screen sizes

## Code Style & Standards
- Follow the existing code style and formatting conventions
- Use the same naming conventions (camelCase for variables, PascalCase for components)
- Maintain consistent file structure and organization
- Use existing patterns for imports, exports, and module organization
- Follow TypeScript best practices as used in the project
- Adhere to ESLint configuration and fix any linting errors

## Best Practices
- Read existing code before writing new code to understand patterns
- Prefer extending existing components over creating new ones
- Reuse existing utilities, hooks, and helper functions
- Maintain existing error handling and validation patterns
- Keep accessibility standards consistent with the rest of the codebase
- Preserve SEO optimizations and metadata structures
- Maintain existing performance optimizations

## When Making Changes
1. **Analyze first**: Understand the existing code structure and design patterns
2. **Preserve**: Keep all existing functionality intact
3. **Match**: Follow the existing design system and visual style
4. **Optimize**: Write efficient, clean, and maintainable code
5. **Test**: Ensure changes don't break existing features

## Project-Specific Notes
- **This is the official debating society club page (Debsoc)**
- This is a Next.js 15 project with TypeScript
- Uses Tailwind CSS v4 for styling
- Uses Framer Motion and GSAP for animations
- Follows the App Router structure
- Maintains existing component patterns and data structures
- Content and features should reflect the debating society's activities, events, and community

